WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:02.070
BROADCOM:
When you think of the largest tech companies

00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:05.029
in the world, what names come to mind?

00:00:05.029 --> 00:00:09.580
Probably Apple, Microsoft, Google, Nvidia,
Amazon, and Meta right?

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:12.410
Well, what about Broadcom?

00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:16.960
Did you know that Broadcom is currently the
9th largest tech company in the world?

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:23.859
In fact, they boast a market cap of $570 billion.

00:00:23.859 --> 00:00:28.240
Broadcom has been one of those background
companies that has grown way larger and way

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:30.500
more powerful that anyone realized.

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:35.989
I mean, just take a look at their stock graph,
it’s basically a rocket ship having grown

00:00:35.989 --> 00:00:39.160
74X since late 2009 alone.

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:44.410
For perspective, even Apple has only grown
32x during that time period, and it’s the

00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:46.940
same story with the rest of big tech as well.

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:53.230
Microsoft only grew 17x, Google only grew
13x, and Facebook has only grown 10x.

00:00:53.230 --> 00:00:58.820
They only big tech company that has really
outshined Broadcom in terms of growth is Nvidia

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:04.589
who’s stock graph is an even bigger rocket
ship having grown 177x during the same time

00:01:04.589 --> 00:01:05.589
period.

00:01:05.589 --> 00:01:10.030
But, there’s one massive difference between
Nvidia and Broadcom.

00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:14.880
For quite some time now, Nvidia has been involved
in multiple super hyped up industries whether

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:19.420
it be crypto mining, self driving cars, or
most recently language models.

00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:24.659
Apple literally created the iPhone, and Google,
Facebook, and Microsoft created or acquired

00:01:24.659 --> 00:01:27.850
services that are used by billions of people.

00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:29.869
But Broadcom on the other hand?

00:01:29.869 --> 00:01:33.579
Well, that’s as boring and background as
it gets.

00:01:33.579 --> 00:01:38.470
That’s why they’ve been able to grow to
nearly being a top 10 company without the

00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:40.159
general public noticing.

00:01:40.159 --> 00:01:44.110
But, that doesn't mean that you should take
Broadcom lightly.

00:01:44.110 --> 00:01:49.509
Broadcom estimates that 99% of all internet
traffic across the entire world passes through

00:01:49.509 --> 00:01:55.860
at least one Broadcom chip, and they’ve
produced quite a few skeletons to get to that

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:56.860
point.

00:01:56.860 --> 00:02:01.510
In fact, just recently, they completed their
acquisition of VMWare for $69 billion which

00:02:01.510 --> 00:02:05.680
has turned into one of the most controversial
acquisitions within the tech world.

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:10.610
So, here’s what you need to know about Broadcom,
the truth about how they got so large, and

00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:15.019
why people in the know are so worried about
Broadcom.

00:02:15.019 --> 00:02:24.530
MAKING A NAME:
Taking a look back, Broadcom has been somewhat

00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:29.269
of a dark horse within the tech industry since
the very beginning way back in 1961.

00:02:29.269 --> 00:02:33.990
One of the main reasons for this is that Broadcom
was never some sort of humble startup that

00:02:33.990 --> 00:02:37.250
was cherished by its consumers at any point
in time.

00:02:37.250 --> 00:02:41.319
Rather, they were started in the shadows and
they stayed in the shadows.

00:02:41.319 --> 00:02:47.130
You see, Broadcom was originally started as
a division of within HP in 1961 called HP

00:02:47.130 --> 00:02:48.130
Associates.

00:02:48.130 --> 00:02:53.769
1961 was very much the stone ages when it
comes to tech world, but by this point, HP

00:02:53.769 --> 00:02:58.360
had already made a name for themselves having
been in business since 1939.

00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:02.380
They were known for making cutting edge audio
oscillators and radar technology that were

00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:06.379
famously used by Disney and the US military
during World War 2 respectively.

00:03:06.379 --> 00:03:12.970
As such, Broadcom, or HP Associates, was launched
with a great pedigree and plenty of resources,

00:03:12.970 --> 00:03:17.269
but for the first 30 years you wouldn’t
have even known that they existed.

00:03:17.269 --> 00:03:21.771
In fact, Broadcom’s own corporate history
page only a few bullet points describing the

00:03:21.771 --> 00:03:26.129
first 30 years of business which really just
centers on 3 inventions.

00:03:26.129 --> 00:03:29.659
The first of these inventions was gallium
aresnide phosphide LEDs.

00:03:29.659 --> 00:03:34.440
These LEDs are what you see at traffic lights
and big LED signs.

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:37.239
Their second invention also had to do with
LEDs.

00:03:37.239 --> 00:03:42.049
They were the ones who created the world’s
first commercially available LED dot matrix

00:03:42.049 --> 00:03:43.049
displays.

00:03:43.049 --> 00:03:47.750
And lastly, moving into the 1970s, they also
created the first fiber optic transmitters

00:03:47.750 --> 00:03:49.299
and receivers.

00:03:49.299 --> 00:03:53.630
These 3 inventions provided them with plenty
of business behind the scenes especially when

00:03:53.630 --> 00:03:55.829
combined with the brand image of HP.

00:03:55.829 --> 00:04:01.080
But, this is not what really made Broadcom
into what it is today.

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:05.939
For that, we have to fast forward to the early
1990s to the early days of the internet.

00:04:05.939 --> 00:04:10.849
You see, while all of the VCs and entrepreneur
hopefuls were piling into the internet space

00:04:10.849 --> 00:04:15.739
hoping to make it big, one professor student
pair named Henry Samueli and Henry Nicholas

00:04:15.739 --> 00:04:18.799
at UCLA would decide to sell the shovels.

00:04:18.799 --> 00:04:23.260
If the internet was truly gonna become the
next big thing, everyone was surely gonna

00:04:23.260 --> 00:04:28.270
need a way to connect and get access to the
network, and this is precisely what this duo

00:04:28.270 --> 00:04:29.419
would address.

00:04:29.419 --> 00:04:34.940
Together they would found a small private
company called Broadcom in 1991 and they would

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:38.130
begin developing integrated circuits that
made the internet.

00:04:38.130 --> 00:04:43.050
If you’ve ever wondered how in the world
it was possible to get an internet connection

00:04:43.050 --> 00:04:47.490
using cable TV lines, well, you can thank
these guys.

00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:52.419
Unlike HP Associates, these guys didn’t
really have a reputation or connections.

00:04:52.419 --> 00:04:56.350
It was really just the quality of their invention
that did all the talking.

00:04:56.350 --> 00:05:00.850
Their first few customers themselves included
the likes of Time Warner, Rockwell International,

00:05:00.850 --> 00:05:03.820
Analog Devices, and even the US Air Force.

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:08.520
These customers alone would provide Broadcom
with over $5 million in annual revenue and

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:12.990
attract the attention of Intel who would loan
the company another $5 million.

00:05:12.990 --> 00:05:17.050
And if you’re wondering what all the hubbub
was about, then I think these numbers will

00:05:17.050 --> 00:05:19.330
really put things in perspective.

00:05:19.330 --> 00:05:25.220
At the time, phone based internet offered
speeds of up to 28.8 kilobits per second.

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:30.360
Broadcom's cable based solutions on the other
hand offered speeds of up to 1000 times that.

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:35.300
And they would soon move over to revolutionizing
the phone internet space as well.

00:05:35.300 --> 00:05:39.819
Hearing all this, you’re probably wondering
where all the controversy is.

00:05:39.819 --> 00:05:44.819
So far, this is relly just a feel good story
about ingenuity and innovation that made the

00:05:44.819 --> 00:05:47.220
whole internet what it is today.

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:51.880
And it definitely could’ve been exactly
that, but pretty soon, our profesor student

00:05:51.880 --> 00:05:57.139
duo would lose their way after seeing big
dollar signs.

00:05:57.139 --> 00:06:07.680
A DARK HORSE:
By 1998, Broadcom would grow to 11,750 employees

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:12.910
across 15 countries and they would go public
with an insanely successful IPO that made

00:06:12.910 --> 00:06:15.120
the founders into centi millionaires.

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:20.190
They could’ve just held onto their stakes
for the long term and they would’ve become

00:06:20.190 --> 00:06:25.830
deca billionaires but what they wanted was
a larger portion of the pie leading us into

00:06:25.830 --> 00:06:30.990
one of the biggest stock scandals of the era:
options backdating.

00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:35.470
Options backdating was an extremely prevalent
scandal throughout the late 90s and early

00:06:35.470 --> 00:06:40.750
2000s and it involved the likes of Apple,
UnitedHealth Group, Staples, The Cheesecake

00:06:40.750 --> 00:06:44.960
Factory, KB Homes, Monster, and of course
Broadcom.

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:46.380
How does it work you ask?

00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:52.400
Well, it was all due to one misguided accounting
rule that was implemented in 1972.

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:56.960
The rule permitted companies to avoid disclosing
executive compensation if it was in the form

00:06:56.960 --> 00:06:58.729
of stock options.

00:06:58.729 --> 00:07:03.389
This wasn’t a big deal at the time or for
the next 25 years really because stock options

00:07:03.389 --> 00:07:06.120
weren’t nearly as popular back in the day.

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:09.910
But that would all change with the rise of
dotcom companies.

00:07:09.910 --> 00:07:15.560
All of sudden, billion dollar fortunes were
being minted overnight thanks to stock compensation.

00:07:15.560 --> 00:07:21.560
So, naturally, stock compensation became far
more desirable than cash compensation and

00:07:21.560 --> 00:07:25.729
pretty soon these executives would discover
a loophole within the law.

00:07:25.729 --> 00:07:29.699
Given that companies didn’t have to disclose
stock option grants to investors at the time

00:07:29.699 --> 00:07:34.419
in which they were granted, the whole system
essentially ran off the honor system.

00:07:34.419 --> 00:07:38.520
It was up to the executives to be honest about
how much stock compensation they received

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:39.740
and when.

00:07:39.740 --> 00:07:44.840
But let’s just say that a lot of tech executives
weren’t exactly honest.

00:07:44.840 --> 00:07:48.949
Essentially, they would team up with their
executive buddies and create false paperwork

00:07:48.949 --> 00:07:53.159
that made it seem like they all received a
bunch of stock grants 5 years ago when the

00:07:53.159 --> 00:07:56.090
stock was 50 times cheaper.

00:07:56.090 --> 00:07:59.910
And it was now up to the investors to pay
out these massive compensations through the

00:07:59.910 --> 00:08:02.590
form of stock dilution.

00:08:02.590 --> 00:08:07.870
This practice would end up costing investors
over $10 billion, $2.2 billion of which was

00:08:07.870 --> 00:08:11.590
“allegedly” due to none other than Broadcom.

00:08:11.590 --> 00:08:15.060
After these revelations come to light, both
founders would step away from the company

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:18.120
but they were never really held accountable.

00:08:18.120 --> 00:08:23.259
The cases against them would eventually be
dropped due to “prosecutorial misconduct”

00:08:23.259 --> 00:08:27.580
and Broadcom would end up settling for just
$160 million.

00:08:27.580 --> 00:08:32.380
Both founders would end up walking away as
deca billionaires but this scandal would forever

00:08:32.380 --> 00:08:37.229
stain Broadcom and turn them into the dark
horse that they are today.

00:08:37.229 --> 00:08:41.959
After this massive scandal, Broadcom very
much needed a way to reset and move forward

00:08:41.959 --> 00:08:46.150
and this is where the other half of Broadcom
would come into play.

00:08:46.150 --> 00:08:49.650
Remember how we started off this story talking
about HP Associates?

00:08:49.650 --> 00:08:52.529
Well, this is where they would become relevant.

00:08:52.529 --> 00:08:57.400
Right before the turn of the century, HP Associates
would break off from HP and become a new company

00:08:57.400 --> 00:09:00.089
called Agilent Technologies.

00:09:00.089 --> 00:09:05.510
This company would then be purchased by KKR
and Silver Lake Partners in 2005 for $2.7

00:09:05.510 --> 00:09:09.620
billion, creating a new company called Avago
Technologies.

00:09:09.620 --> 00:09:13.990
Avago lurk around the shadows of the tech
world for the next decade before they finally

00:09:13.990 --> 00:09:16.600
made their big move in 2015.

00:09:16.600 --> 00:09:21.630
They would agree to move forward and acquire
the entirety of the stained Broadcom for $37

00:09:21.630 --> 00:09:25.580
billion bringing us into the modern day Broadcom.

00:09:25.580 --> 00:09:34.620
A SHADOW GIANT:
Honestly, there’s really nothing good to

00:09:34.620 --> 00:09:38.620
say about the modern Broadcom which is such
a shame.

00:09:38.620 --> 00:09:43.860
These guys are literally responsible for creating
the tech that makes the modern internet possible,

00:09:43.860 --> 00:09:47.450
yet their behavior really makes it hard to
root for them.

00:09:47.450 --> 00:09:52.410
For starters, it appears that they're patent
trolls.

00:09:52.410 --> 00:09:55.930
In 2017, for example, they sued smart TV makers
for patent infringement.

00:09:55.930 --> 00:10:00.320
And in 2020, they sued Netflix also for patent
infringement.

00:10:00.320 --> 00:10:05.440
Though the tech community at large just views
these lawsuits as Broadcom suing because their

00:10:05.440 --> 00:10:06.550
jealous.

00:10:06.550 --> 00:10:10.161
Even the judge that was ruling on the case
said quote “You’re killing me” calling

00:10:10.161 --> 00:10:12.399
the filings “massive overkill”.

00:10:12.399 --> 00:10:16.260
But, their patent tantrums are the least of
your worries.

00:10:16.260 --> 00:10:21.490
The far bigger issue is that Broadcom controls
the entire internet chip market.

00:10:21.490 --> 00:10:25.390
Calling them a monopoly is honestly understating
the situation.

00:10:25.390 --> 00:10:31.050
Again keep in mind, according to Broadcom
themselves, 99% of all internet traffic passes

00:10:31.050 --> 00:10:36.450
through their chips, so basically every company
that you can think of in the entire world

00:10:36.450 --> 00:10:42.389
is reliant on Broadcom, and I’m not just
talking about tech companies.

00:10:42.389 --> 00:10:46.030
Broadcom has fortune 500 customers in all
of these industries.

00:10:46.030 --> 00:10:51.760
And let’s just say Broadcom is very much
a predatory service provider for these customers.

00:10:51.760 --> 00:10:56.279
You know, things like predatory contracts,
retaliation on non loyal customers, exclusivity

00:10:56.279 --> 00:10:57.730
arrangements and so on.

00:10:57.730 --> 00:11:02.010
I mean, you can’t feel too bad for these
Fortune 500 customers because they usually

00:11:02.010 --> 00:11:06.850
turn around and do the exact same thing to
us, but the point still stands that Broadcom

00:11:06.850 --> 00:11:10.300
is very much a toxic force within the tech
world.

00:11:10.300 --> 00:11:15.940
They control an invention that basically everyone
needs and they leverage that to the max.

00:11:15.940 --> 00:11:20.240
And more recently, they’ve been looking
to expand their monopoly to adjacent sectors.

00:11:20.240 --> 00:11:26.000
In 2017, for example, they would attempt a
hostile takeover of the mobile chipmaker Qualcomm

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:28.840
for $105 billion.

00:11:28.840 --> 00:11:33.829
The only reason this deal didn’t go through
is because President Trump would block it.

00:11:33.829 --> 00:11:38.089
But, it’s not like Broadcom was really all
that upset about it because they would just

00:11:38.089 --> 00:11:40.250
move on to their next acquisition target.

00:11:40.250 --> 00:11:45.230
In mid 2018, for example, Broadcom would go
ahead and purchase CA technologies, a tech

00:11:45.230 --> 00:11:49.880
infrastructure giant, for $18.9 billion in
cash.

00:11:49.880 --> 00:11:55.480
And most recently, they completed their acquisition
of VMWare for $69 billion.

00:11:55.480 --> 00:12:00.560
Every time that Broadcom completes an acquisition,
they make sure to spread their toxicity, and

00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:05.070
that’s why much of the tech community has
basically just accepted that VMWare is basically

00:12:05.070 --> 00:12:06.080
dead.

00:12:06.080 --> 00:12:12.060
It’s very similar to what GE was doing back
in the 80s and 90s but instead of it happening

00:12:12.060 --> 00:12:16.160
in the industrial world, it’s happening
in the tech world.

00:12:16.160 --> 00:12:21.350
And honestly, the only way that this will
likely end is by Broadcom becoming so big

00:12:21.350 --> 00:12:25.550
and so toxic that they crumble from the inside
out.

00:12:25.550 --> 00:12:31.860
Until then though, they’re very much a force
to be reckoned with and that is the truth

00:12:31.860 --> 00:12:33.750
about Broadcom.

00:12:33.750 --> 00:12:38.670
Someone else who has been investing a bunch
into acquisitions is none other than Microsoft,

00:12:38.670 --> 00:12:40.260
and it may end up backfiring.

00:12:40.260 --> 00:12:45.550
Check out this video to learn more.

